WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Secure data access begins with parameterized patterns.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:16.040
Whether the application uses Entity Framework, Dapper, ADO.NET, or another data layer, values should be passed as parameters rather than assembled into query text.

3
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:24.380
ORMs reduce some risks, but they do not remove the need to review query shape, authorization, filtering, tenant boundaries, and performance limits.

4
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.220
Data access and authorization often meet at the object level.

5
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.160
A query should not only be syntactically safe; it should return records the current identity is allowed to see.

6
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:41.740
Filters for tenant, ownership, status, workflow, or scope should be deliberate and tested.

7
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.560
Avoid relying on later UI code to hide records that should not have been returned.

8
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:54.520
File handling is security-sensitive because files combine input, storage, metadata, user expectations, and downstream processing.

9
00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:05.660
Uploads should have limits for size, type, name handling, storage location, scanning or review processes where appropriate, and separation from executable paths.

10
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.360
Download behavior should also enforce authorization and avoid trusting user-supplied paths.

11
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.560
Path handling deserves care.

12
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:27.500
Normalize paths before making decisions, avoid combining untrusted values into sensitive paths without validation, generate server-side storage names when possible, and keep user-facing names separate from storage identifiers.

13
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.040
A display name does not need to be the filesystem name.

14
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.520
Deserialization and parsing are boundaries.

15
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:41.660
Imported files, messages, serialized objects, and documents should be treated as untrusted input.

16
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.800
The application should prefer safe parsers, bounded processing, expected schemas, and clear rejection behavior.

17
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:54.820
Avoid dangerous dynamic behavior when processing data that came from outside the trust boundary.

18
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.900
The practical goal is controlled data flow.

19
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:10.080
Data should enter through a boundary, be validated, be authorized, be stored safely, and be processed by code paths that do not grant more meaning to the data than it has earned.
